WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.200
In this painting, almost everything is darkness.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.600
The trees twist over a garden&nbsp;
swallowed by the night. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.000
ground can barely be made out. And in the center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.200
wrapped in a black cloak that seems to dissolve&nbsp;
into the shadows, there is a man kneeling.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.700
Only two things about him can be seen clearly: his&nbsp;
face and his clasped hands. The moonlight falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.500
on him from the side, barely lighting his eyes&nbsp;
and his beard. His hair hangs loose and tangled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.400
as if it were soaking wet. And his&nbsp;
gaze is not directed toward the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.000
but straight ahead, as if he were looking at us.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.300
This mysterious man is Jesus. And what we&nbsp;
are seeing is the night before his death.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.200
In the history of art, there are thousands of&nbsp;
paintings that portray this moment. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.600
none of them shows him as alone, as human,&nbsp;
and as immersed in darkness as this one.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.400
This painting is called Christ in the Garden&nbsp;
of Gethsemane. It was painted by the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:15.400
artist Nikolai Ge in 1869, and today it is&nbsp;
held at the Tretyakov Gallery in Moscow.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:21.100
Something impressive about this work is its size.&nbsp;
It stands more than two and a half meters tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.800
so it is made for the viewer to encounter the&nbsp;
figure of Christ practically at life size.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.700
The scene depicts Jesus’s last night as a free&nbsp;
man. According to the Gospels, after the Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.500
Supper, Jesus withdrew to pray in an olive grove&nbsp;
called Gethsemane, knowing he was about to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:44.900
betrayed. And in that moment, he asked God, if it&nbsp;
were possible, to take that fate away from him.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:50.200
Meanwhile, his disciples, whom he had asked&nbsp;
to keep watch with him, had fallen asleep.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:57.200
For centuries, painters depicted this scene in&nbsp;
almost the same way. Jesus appears on his knees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.093
with his eyes turned toward the sky.&nbsp;
An angel descends to comfort him. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.093 --> 00:02:07.993
disciples sleep in the foreground. And in the&nbsp;
distance, Judas approaches with the soldiers.

00:02:07.993 --> 00:02:10.793
In this work, however, we see none of that.

00:02:10.793 --> 00:02:14.193
In this version, there is no angel&nbsp;
coming to comfort him. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.193 --> 00:02:17.293
no disciples around him. There are no soldiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.293 --> 00:02:21.257
and there is nothing that reminds us that&nbsp;
this scene is part of a sacred story. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.257 --> 00:02:21.893
if we want to put it simply, all we see in the&nbsp;
painting is a man, a garden, and the night.

00:02:21.893 --> 00:02:26.393
Now, because of how dark the painting&nbsp;
is, it is hard to make out at first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:32.193
glance. But in a clearer image of the work,&nbsp;
we can see that Jesus is actually kneeling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:37.293
just as the Gospels describe. And yet, there&nbsp;
is nothing in his posture that recalls the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.293 --> 00:02:42.793
traditional depictions. He does not raise his&nbsp;
arms, nor does he look up to the sky in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:48.193
gesture of pleading. His figure is simply&nbsp;
there, with one hand resting on his knee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.193 --> 00:02:54.193
gazing into the darkness. As if the prayer had&nbsp;
already ended, and all that remained was to wait.

00:02:54.793 --> 00:02:59.693
But to understand why Ge painted Jesus this&nbsp;
way, we need to know a little about his story.

00:03:00.293 --> 00:03:07.793
Nikolai Ge was born in 1831 in Voronezh, Russia,&nbsp;
into a noble family of French origin. He began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.793 --> 00:03:14.593
by studying science, but in 1850 he left it all&nbsp;
behind to enter the Imperial Academy of Arts.

00:03:14.593 --> 00:03:22.293
In 1857 he went to Italy, and in Rome he met&nbsp;
Alexander Ivanov, a Russian painter obsessed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.293 --> 00:03:28.893
the figure of Christ, who would leave a deep mark&nbsp;
on him. From 1860 he settled in Florence, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.893 --> 00:03:35.993
he painted his own version of The Last Supper. And&nbsp;
when he presented it in Saint Petersburg in 1863,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.993 --> 00:03:40.493
it made such an impression that the&nbsp;
Academy immediately named him a professor.

00:03:40.493 --> 00:03:45.493
But that success was short-lived. His next&nbsp;
religious paintings were not well received,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.493 --> 00:03:48.293
and the Academy even refused to exhibit them.

00:03:48.293 --> 00:03:53.193
So it was in this context, while still in&nbsp;
Florence, that Nikolai Ge painted this work.

00:03:54.393 --> 00:03:59.693
Now, when we return to the painting, the first&nbsp;
thing that stands out is how the light is used.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.193 --> 00:04:05.394
The moon lights up the branches of the trees,&nbsp;
part of the path, and the rock on the right. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.394 --> 00:04:11.294
on the body of Christ, the light barely falls&nbsp;
at all. His dark cloak blends into the night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.294 --> 00:04:16.194
to the point that it is hard to tell where&nbsp;
his figure ends and the garden begins.

00:04:16.194 --> 00:04:21.294
The only things spared from the darkness are&nbsp;
his face and one of his hands. The same hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:25.794
he has probably just been praying with, and&nbsp;
the face of a man who knows he is about to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.794 --> 00:04:29.694
betrayed in a matter of moments, and&nbsp;
who can do nothing against his fate.

00:04:30.494 --> 00:04:35.794
If we look closely at his hair, we can see that&nbsp;
it falls long, heavy, and tangled, as if it were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.794 --> 00:04:41.394
wet. We might think it is simply the dew of the&nbsp;
night. But the Gospel of Luke describes that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.394 --> 00:04:46.094
during that prayer, Jesus’s anguish was&nbsp;
so great that his sweat fell like great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.094 --> 00:04:51.194
drops of blood. So it is possible that,&nbsp;
with this detail, Ge wanted to capture in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.194 --> 00:04:55.294
his body the feeling of someone who has just&nbsp;
gone through the hardest night of his life.

00:04:56.194 --> 00:05:02.894
Finally, Ge leaves the path behind him open and&nbsp;
illuminated. The moonlight traces a trail that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.894 --> 00:05:08.294
crosses the garden, and although we do not know&nbsp;
whether he left it this way on purpose, it is hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.294 --> 00:05:14.694
not to imagine that along that very path, in just&nbsp;
a few minutes, Judas and the soldiers will arrive.

00:05:15.394 --> 00:05:22.194
Now, this painting was first exhibited in 1869&nbsp;
at the International Art Exhibition in Munich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.194 --> 00:05:27.494
and from that moment on, there was someone&nbsp;
in Russia who never took his eyes off it.

00:05:27.494 --> 00:05:33.194
Pavel Tretyakov, the great collector who would&nbsp;
later found the gallery that bears his name today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.194 --> 00:05:39.594
closely followed the creation of this work. But it&nbsp;
took him almost twenty years to buy it. He finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.594 --> 00:05:46.294
did so in 1887, and it became the first painting&nbsp;
by Ge on a New Testament theme in his collection.

00:05:46.894 --> 00:05:52.094
And here there is a curious detail. Before&nbsp;
sending it to Moscow, Ge went back to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.094 --> 00:05:57.194
on it and made some changes. So almost&nbsp;
two decades after beginning this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.194 --> 00:06:00.794
he still had not finished figuring&nbsp;
out how to paint Christ in this scene.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:05.094
By this time, Nikolai Ge was a different man.

00:06:05.094 --> 00:06:10.495
After returning to Russia, some of his historical&nbsp;
works were successful, but many others were met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.496 --> 00:06:15.696
with indifference, and he took it very hard.&nbsp;
He ended up withdrawing to live on a farm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.696 --> 00:06:20.496
convinced that a man should live from working&nbsp;
the land, and that art should not be sold.

00:06:21.096 --> 00:06:25.996
During those years he became friends with&nbsp;
Leo Tolstoy, whose ideas about faith deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.996 --> 00:06:31.696
influenced him. And his later paintings of&nbsp;
Christ became increasingly raw. So much so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.696 --> 00:06:36.696
that they became a problem. One of his&nbsp;
most famous works, “What Is Truth?”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:42.396
in which he portrays Christ and Pilate,&nbsp;
was removed from an exhibition in 1890.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.396 --> 00:06:47.496
And his painting “The Crucifixion” was&nbsp;
banned by Tsar Alexander III himself.

00:06:48.096 --> 00:06:52.596
In short, knowing the story of this&nbsp;
artist makes us think that Ge never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.596 --> 00:06:56.896
wanted to paint a Christ who followed&nbsp;
Christian tradition, but rather a man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.896 --> 00:07:02.696
who suffers. And in this Gethsemane, we can&nbsp;
already see the beginning of that search.

00:07:02.696 --> 00:07:06.696
What makes this painting so dark is&nbsp;
not only the night that covers it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.696 --> 00:07:12.296
but the absence we feel in it. There are&nbsp;
no angels, no disciples awake, and no light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.296 --> 00:07:17.596
coming from heaven. Only a man kneeling in&nbsp;
the night, waiting for them to come for him.

00:07:17.596 --> 00:07:21.896
And that is why this work continues to&nbsp;
move people so deeply. Because instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.896 --> 00:07:27.296
showing us a God, it shows us a man who,&nbsp;
on his last night, was completely alone.

00:07:29.596 --> 00:07:34.196
I would love to know what you feel when you look&nbsp;
at this painting. Let me know in the comments.

